entityDescription:
Ellinogermaniki Agogi (EA) is an educational organization of private law, officially recognized by the state. EA was the first Greek educational organization, which applied Open and Distance Learning in secondary level education in the year of 1993.
Since 1998, the organization has established a devoted department, the Research and Development (R&D) Department for the design, development and implementation of research activities in education, expanding the collaboration with Universities and Research Institutions across Europe. The R&D Department, which currently employs 13 full time researchers, acts as interface between the pedagogical research, the technological innovation and the school community.
Since its foundation, the R&D department has been actively involved in more than 11 European projects dedicated to Astronomy or Space exploration as well as many more which were partly related to these two fields. In 2012 EA worked with ESA&rsquo;s education department to intergrade ESA&rsquo;s interactive applications in the Go-Lab(https://cordis.europa.eu/project/id/317601)platform. As part of the EU Space Awarenessproject(https://cordis.europa.eu/project/id/638653)consortium, EA worked alongside ESA&rsquo;s education representative and numerous ESERO offices designing and delivering e-learning resources for educators and educational resources for students. Following Space Awareness, EA was also part of the SpaceEU project(https://cordis.europa.eu/project/id/821832)in which ESA Education was a member of the advisory board. EA is familiar with ESA&rsquo;s educational content as it has been making use of them for many years not only internally in the school but also in the framework of EU projects and teacher training courses. EA also has a long experience in designing and organizing teacher training workshops and courses on Astronomy and Space exploration.
The work of the R&D Department also focuses on the following areas:
a) the development of methodologies and empirical research to investigate processes of learning and knowledge acquisition in various subject-matter areas (physics, mathematics, biology, history, etc.);
b) the collaboration with computer science departments and artificial intelligence labs for the development of computational models and AI learning systems;
c) the collaboration with Universities and private companies for the development and testing of educational software;
d) the design of technology-supported learning and e-learning environments.
